  "image_name": "2b4dff028183c962a3fe653e9663574f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the sequence leading to graft-versus-host disease after allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. It starts with a recipient undergoing a conditioning regimen, shown as damaging treatment such as total body irradiation, chemotherapy, or antibodies. After this preparative step, donor hematopoietic stem cells are infused into the recipient as part of allo-HSCT.\n\nThe key message is that, alongside the transplant process, donor immune cells can become alloreactive and attack the recipient’s tissues, producing GVHD. On the right, the recipient is shown with multiple target organs highlighted, emphasizing that GVHD is a systemic complication rather than a single-organ disease. The most prominently affected sites include the skin, liver, and gastrointestinal tract, with additional involvement of the thymus, central nervous system, hematopoietic system, and reproductive organs (ovary or testis).\n\nOverall, the image presents a cause-and-effect pathway: pre-transplant conditioning injures the host, donor cells are introduced, and donor T cells can then react against host tissues, leading to widespread organ damage characteristic of GVHD. The arrows and body schematics make the progression from treatment to transplant to immune-mediated disease visually clear.",
